.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt BIO_S_FILE 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BIO_s_file ,
.Nm BIO_new_file ,
.Nm BIO_new_fp ,
.Nm BIO_set_fp ,
.Nm BIO_get_fp ,
.Nm BIO_read_filename ,
.Nm BIO_write_filename ,
.Nm BIO_append_filename ,
.Nm BIO_rw_filename
.Nd FILE bio
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/bio.h
.Ft const BIO_METHOD *
.Fo BIO_s_file
.Fa void
.Fc
.Ft BIO *
.Fo BIO_new_file
.Fa "const char *filename"
.Fa "const char *mode"
.Fc
.Ft BIO *
.Fo BIO_new_fp
.Fa "FILE *stream"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fc
.Bd -literal
 BIO_set_fp(BIO *b, FILE *fp, int flags);
 BIO_get_fp(BIO *b, FILE **fpp);
.Ed
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_read_filename
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "char *name"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_write_filename
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "char *name"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_append_filename
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "char *name"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_rw_filename
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "char *name"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn BIO_s_file
returns the BIO file method.
As its name implies it is a wrapper round the stdio FILE structure and
it is a source/sink BIO.
.Pp
Calls to
.Xr BIO_read_ex 3
and
.Xr BIO_write_ex 3
read and write data to the underlying stream.
.Xr BIO_gets 3
and
.Xr BIO_puts 3
are supported on file BIOs.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_flush 3
on a file BIO calls the
.Xr fflush 3
function on the wrapped stream.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_reset 3
attempts to change the file pointer to the start of file using
fseek(stream, 0, 0).
.Pp
.Xr BIO_seek 3
sets the file pointer to position
.Sy ofs
from start of file using fseek(stream, ofs, 0).
.Pp
.Xr BIO_eof 3
calls
.Xr feof 3 .
.Pp
Setting the BIO_CLOSE flag calls
.Xr fclose 3
on the stream when the BIO is freed.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_new_file
creates a new file BIO with mode
.Fa mode
the meaning of
.Fa mode
is the same as the stdio function
.Xr fopen 3 .
The BIO_CLOSE flag is set on the returned BIO.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_new_fp
creates a file BIO wrapping
.Fa stream .
Flags can be: BIO_CLOSE, BIO_NOCLOSE (the close flag) BIO_FP_TEXT (sets
the underlying stream to text mode, default is binary: this only has any
effect under Win32).
.Pp
.Xr BIO_set_fp 3
sets the fp of a file BIO to
.Sy fp .
.Fa flags
has the same meaning as in
.Fn BIO_new_fp ,
it is a macro.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_get_fp 3
retrieves the fp of a file BIO, it is a macro.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_seek 3
is a macro that sets the position pointer to
.Sy offset
bytes from the start of file.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_tell 3
returns the value of the position pointer.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_read_filename ,
.Fn BIO_write_filename ,
.Fn BIO_append_filename
and
.Fn BIO_rw_filename
set the file BIO
.Fa b
to use file
.Fa name
for reading, writing, append or read write respectively.
.Sh NOTES
When wrapping stdout, stdin or stderr the underlying stream should not
normally be closed so the BIO_NOCLOSE flag should be set.
.Pp
Because the file BIO calls the underlying stdio functions any quirks in
stdio behaviour will be mirrored by the corresponding BIO.
.Pp
On Windows BIO_new_files reserves for the filename argument to be UTF-8
encoded.
In other words if you have to make it work in multi- lingual
environment, encode filenames in UTF-8.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn BIO_s_file
returns the file BIO method.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_new_file
and
.Fn BIO_new_fp
return a file BIO or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_set_fp 3
and
.Xr BIO_get_fp 3
return 1 for success or <=0 for failure (although the current
implementation never return 0).
.Pp
.Xr BIO_seek 3
returns 0 for success or negative values for failure.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_tell 3
returns the current file position or negative values for failure.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_read_filename ,
.Fn BIO_write_filename ,
.Fn BIO_append_filename
and
.Fn BIO_rw_filename
return 1 for success or <=0 for failure.
An error is also returned if the file does not exist.
.Sh EXAMPLES
File BIO "hello world":
.Bd -literal
 BIO *bio_out;

 bio_out = BIO_new_fp(stdout, BIO_NOCLOSE);
 BIO_printf(bio_out, "Hello World\en");
.Ed
.Pp
Alternative technique:
.Bd -literal
 BIO *bio_out;

 bio_out = BIO_new(BIO_s_file());
 if (bio_out == NULL)
     /* Error */
 if (BIO_set_fp(bio_out, stdout, BIO_NOCLOSE) <= 0)
     /* Error */
 BIO_printf(bio_out, "Hello World\en");
.Ed
.Pp
Write to a file:
.Bd -literal
 BIO *out;

 out = BIO_new_file("filename.txt", "w");
 if (!out)
     /* Error */
 BIO_printf(out, "Hello World\en");
 BIO_free(out);
.Ed
.Pp
Alternative technique:
.Bd -literal
 BIO *out;

 out = BIO_new(BIO_s_file());
 if (out == NULL)
     /* Error */
 if (BIO_write_filename(out, "filename.txt") <= 0)
     /* Error */
 BIO_printf(out, "Hello World\en");
 BIO_free(out);
.Ed
.Sh BUGS
.Xr BIO_reset 3
and
.Xr BIO_seek 3
are implemented using
.Xr fseek 3
on the underlying stream.
The return value for
.Xr fseek 3
is 0 for success or -1 if an error occurred this differs from other
types of BIO which will typically return 1 for success and a non
positive value if an error occurred.
